  "investigation_notes": "Uploads failing due to storage - inode exhaustion, not app or DB.\n\n**Evidence:**\n\n* App `POST /upload` returns `500 Internal Server Error` with `OSError: [Errno 28] No space left on device: '/data/tmp/...part'` (E3)\n* App `/health` check fails same way trying to write `'/data/uploads/.health-...'` with `OSError: [Errno 28] No space left on device`, returning `503 Service Unavailable` (E3). External health probe also `503 {\"status\":\"unhealthy\"}` (E2).\n* Both containers `running`: `incidentlab-app-1 Up 21 seconds`, `incidentlab-db-1 Up 27 seconds (healthy)` (E1). App `running:true, exit_code:0, oom_killed:false, restart_count:0` (E7).\n\n**Root cause - inodes exhausted on upload filesystem:**\n\n* App mount: `bind source: /srv/lab-data destination: /data rw:true` (E7), so `/data/tmp` and `/data/uploads` in (E3) are `/srv/lab-data` on host.\n* Filesystem holding `/srv/lab-data`: `use_percent:37.3, available_mb:283.0` but `inode_use_percent:100.0` (E5).\n* That explains `ENOSPC` despite free block space: no inodes left to create new files.\n* Root filesystem is healthy: `use_percent:6.8, inode_use_percent:1.5` (E6), so problem is isolated to `/srv/lab-data` volume.\n\n**Ruled out:**\n\n* Permissions: `/srv/lab-data/uploads owner 1000:1000 mode 0755, app_uid 1000, app_user_can_write:true` (E8). Parent `/srv/lab-data` same `owner 1000:1000, app_user_can_write:true` (E11).\n* Block-capacity full: ruled out by `used_mb:168.4 / available_mb:283.0` (E5).\n* DB dependency: `incidentlab-db-1` `running:true, health:healthy` (E9), logs `database system is ready to accept connections` with no errors (E4), `pg_isready exit_code 0` repeatedly (E10).\n* Crash/restart loop: `restart_count:0` for app (E7) and DB (E9), events show only one clean recreate then `start`, no `die` with error (E10).\n\n**Conclusion:**\nUploads stopped because every new file creation on `/data` fails with `ENOSPC` (E3) caused by `100% inode use` on its host filesystem `/srv/lab-data` (E5), despite `37.3%` block use and `writable` permissions (E8,E11). Health `503` (E2) is a symptom of the same failed write probe (E3).\n",
